sections.body_md:
Before 1789 Europe had **no nation-states**: dynastic empires such as the **Habsburg Empire** held together peoples of many languages only through loyalty to the ruler.

The **French Revolution (1789)** transferred **sovereignty from the monarch to the people**. To create a collective identity the revolutionaries:
- spread the ideas of **la patrie** (fatherland) and **le citoyen** (citizen): one community of equal citizens governed by a constitution;
- adopted a **tricolour flag** in place of the royal standard;
- turned the Estates General into the elected **National Assembly**;
- composed new hymns, took oaths and commemorated martyrs in the name of the nation;
- set up a **centralised administration** with **uniform laws**, abolished **internal customs duties**, and adopted a uniform system of **weights and measures**;
- discouraged regional dialects and made **the French of Paris** the common language.

They saw it as France's task to **free other European peoples** from autocratic rule. Students and the middle classes formed **Jacobin clubs** elsewhere, and French armies carried nationalism abroad in the 1790s.
